.TH NFS-SMARTMOUNT 8 "2026-11-13" "smart-mount 1.7" "System Administration Manual"
.SH NAME
nfs-smartmount \- mount NFS and CIFS shares only on the right network, from the
right server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nfs-smartmount.sh
.RB show " ["
.B \-\-json
.RB "]"
.br
.B nfs-smartmount.sh
.RB { mount | unmount | apply }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nfs-smartmount.sh
mounts each configured share only when the machine is on a network where the
server is reachable
.I and
identified, and unmounts it otherwise.
It works for NFS and CIFS (SMB) shares, any number of them, each with its own
mount point.
Nothing is read from
.I /etc/fstab
at run time: the tool mounts each share itself.
.PP
Identity is verified at layer 2, not by host name or address alone: the server's
MAC address is read from the ARP table of the link in use, and compared with the
expected MAC of that share.
A share without a target MAC is never mounted.
.PP
All the policy lives in one place.
.B apply
decides for every share and is the only verb that mounts or unmounts anything on
its own initiative.
The two NetworkManager dispatcher hooks and the systemd timer only call it, so
they cannot undo each other's work when both links are up (docked laptop, Wi-Fi
on a different network).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B show
Print, for every configured share, its mount point, source, state
.RI ( "monté, sain" ,
.I "monté, MUET"
when the mount no longer answers, or
.IR démonté )
and the options retained in the configuration.
Any NFS or CIFS mount that does not come from the configuration is listed as
.IR "monté (hors configuration)" .
Read-only: nothing is mounted, unmounted or logged.
It also works without root: the configuration is then unreadable, only the real
mounts are listed, and a note says so.
.TP
.BI show " \-\-json"
Same information as JSON, for scripts and graphical front ends.
Each share entry carries
.IR type ,
.IR server ,
.IR share ,
.IR mountpoint ,
.IR networks ,
.IR source ,
.IR fstype ,
.I options
as retained by the user,
.I runtime_options
as reported by the kernel,
.IR state ,
.I mounted
and
.IR healthy .
.TP
.B apply
Evaluate the policy for every share and mount, repair or unmount as required.
This is what the timer and the hooks call.
.TP
.B mount
Mount the shares that are legitimately reachable; never unmount anything.
.TP
.B unmount
Unmount every configured mount point.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.I /etc/smart-mount/config
is a shell file, read as root, mode 0640 root:root.
One record per share, seven or eight fields separated by
.BR | :
.PP
.RS
type|server|share|mount point|options|mac|credentials|networks
.RE
.PP
.TP
.I type
.B nfs
or
.BR cifs .
.TP
.I server
IP address or host name of the server.
.TP
.I share
Exported path for NFS, share name for CIFS.
.TP
.I options
Passed as
.BI \-o " options"
to
.BR mount (8).
Shown by
.BR show .
.TP
.I mac
MAC address of the server.
Mandatory: a share without one is refused with exit status 4.
.TP
.I credentials
CIFS only: path of a credentials file, passed as
.IR credentials= .
Never as
.IR \-o " username=...,password=..." ,
because a command line is readable by every user through
.BR ps (1).
.TP
.I networks
Optional: networks where this share may mount, separated by
.B ;
or
.BR , .
Empty means the global
.BR WIFI_NAME .
A single
.B *
means any network, the MAC alone deciding.
.PP
Global variables:
.BR WIFI_NAME ,
.BR WIFI_IFACE ,
.B LAN_IFACE .
.PP
A configuration file from an earlier version (flat
.BR NFS_SERVER_IP ,
.BR MOUNT_POINT ", ...)"
is still read as a single NFS share.
.SH NETWORK IDENTITY
Wi-Fi is accepted when the SSID is allowed for the share
.I and
the server's MAC is confirmed on
.BR WIFI_IFACE .
Ethernet is accepted when the server's MAC is confirmed on
.BR LAN_IFACE .
If either link is a match, the share is mounted; if both links are ruled out
without ambiguity, it is unmounted; if a link cannot be read, nothing is done

.IP
failed read must never destroy a working mount.
.PP
Anything that changes the MAC seen by this host invalidates the check: a server
behind a router or a VLAN, a VPN, NAT.
Configure the MAC that is actually visible on the link.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/smart-mount/config
Share definitions, read as root.
.TP
.I /etc/smart-mount/credentials-*
CIFS credentials, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/nfs-smartmount.sh
The program.
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/98-eth-nfs-mac
Ethernet hook.
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/99-wifi-nfs-mount
Wi-Fi hook.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/nfs-network-check.{service,timer}
Periodic policy run, every 15 minutes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_DRY_RUN
When set to
.BR 1 ,
evaluate and log the decision for every share without mounting or unmounting.
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file.
Overrides
.IR /etc/smart-mount/config ;
intended for tests.
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_DEPS
Space separated list replacing the runtime dependency check.
Intended for tests.
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_GROUP
Group owning the configuration file, used by
.BR setup.sh .
Defaults to
.BR root .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a deliberate decision not to mount.
.TP
.B 1
A share could not be mounted.
.TP
.B 2
A mount point could not be unmounted.
.TP
.B 3
Invalid usage, or an invalid share record.
.TP
.B 4
A share has no target MAC.
.TP
.B 5
A runtime dependency is missing; the message names it.
.TP
.B 122
Configuration missing, or no share configured.
.TP
.B 127
The server was unreachable before mounting.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what is mounted, with the options retained for each share:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ sudo nfs-smartmount.sh show
TYPE  POINT DE MONTAGE  SOURCE                             ETAT         OPTIONS
nfs   /media/NAS        192.168.50.85:/media/diskusb/data  monté, sain  nodev,nosuid,noexec
cifs  /media/NAS-smb    //192.168.50.85/usbdisk_data       monté, sain  guest,uid=1000,gid=1000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the policy for every share without touching anything:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ sudo SMARTMOUNT_DRY_RUN=1 nfs-smartmount.sh apply
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed a graphical front end:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ nfs-smartmount.sh show --json | jq '.shares[] | {mountpoint, state}'
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The MAC check assumes the server is on the same layer 2 segment.
A cold ARP cache needs a ping to fill; a server that filters ICMP and is not
already in the ARP table cannot be confirmed, so its share will not mount.
NFS shares mounted with
.B hard
can freeze a process for as long as the RPC timeouts last; the hooks unmount on
network loss and the timer repairs dead mounts, but a hung mount is only forcibly
released on NFS
.RB ( "umount \-f" ).
One network per machine: the SSID and both interface names are global, not per
share.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR umount (8),
.BR findmnt (8),
.BR nfs (5),
.BR mount.cifs (8),
.BR NetworkManager (8),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
